00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:29.200
Now, let's look at the main file that actually holds the flow,

122
00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:33.200
that is inside ourr SRC deep research flow folder.

123
00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:35.760
So if you open that main file,

124
00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:38.160
you will see that we create the flow state.

125
00:07:38.799 --> 00:07:42.399
This is the state that is going to be shared across the entire flow.

126
00:07:42.399 --> 00:07:45.200
So as all the functions are being executed,

127
00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:50.559
we're going to be populating this data so we can exchange data between the functions.

128
00:07:50.559 --> 00:07:56.880
So we can read and write into the state at any point during the flow execution.

129
00:07:56.880 --> 00:07:59.440
Another interesting thing that I want to pinpoint

130
00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:03.200
is that we're importing this persist decorator.

131
00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:07.119
And that's a new decorator that I want to highlight into this code real quick.

132
00:08:07.119 --> 00:08:14.000
What it does is it persists the state and every function state after flow.

133
00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:18.399
So every function that gets executed by the end of that,

134
00:08:18.399 --> 00:08:21.760
it saves that state into your local database.

135
00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:24.480
So it can reuse that state later

136
00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:28.720
if you trigger the same flow with that same ID once again,

137
00:08:29.279 --> 00:08:35.119
meaning that you can use that state to persist data across many different executions.

138
00:08:35.679 --> 00:08:41.440
So let's now go into our actual flow where we have the persist decorator

139
00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:44.640
and where we're not only inheriting from the flow class,

140
00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:48.159
but we're being very clear that it's using the research state.

141
00:08:48.719 --> 00:08:53.359
Our first function is exactly the one that we showed on the slides just a second ago,

142
00:08:53.359 --> 00:08:58.640
where we have the start conversation function that is annotated with the start decorator.

143
00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:00.880
And here you can see that we do some prints,

144
00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:04.400
just that we can see that in the terminal when we execute that in a second.

145
00:09:05.039 --> 00:09:08.400
We check if there was a query before this.

146
00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:11.919
And this just means that if we executed this flow before,

147
00:09:11.919 --> 00:09:14.080
because we are persisting the state,

148
00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:17.599
and you're going to see that once that we actually run it in the terminal.

149
00:09:17.599 --> 00:09:22.400
And if there was a state before, meaning that we executed this flow before,

150
00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:26.320
it's going to tell us what was the last thing that we researched about,

151
00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:28.960
because that will be stored in the state.

152
00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:32.799
I want to stop here real quick to explain how useful this can be.

153
00:09:32.799 --> 00:09:38.000
Let's say that you're creating a flow that will handle many turns of conversations,

154
00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:40.000
where whenever you run the flow,

155
00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:45.200
you want that same conversation to keep going and that same data to be in there.

156
00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:48.239
Maybe you want to make sure that this flow remembers

157
00:09:48.239 --> 00:09:52.880
every single research that it has ever made as you go talk with it.

158
00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:57.760
Well, if you want to do that, you could actually create a variable in the state

159
00:09:57.760 --> 00:09:59.840
that will hold all that data.

160
00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:02.559
And as long as you're persisting that data,

161
00:10:02.559 --> 00:10:07.919
you will always be able to load it back up when you run the flow again,

162
00:10:07.919 --> 00:10:10.799
as long as you're passing the same flow ID.

163
00:10:10.799 --> 00:10:12.960
We're going to see that in practice in a second.

164
00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:14.559
So don't be scared away.

165
00:10:14.559 --> 00:10:16.479
We're going to show you how that actually works.

166
00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:20.159
After this initial function, where we ask the customer

167
00:10:20.159 --> 00:10:22.400
what it is that it wants to know about,

168
00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:25.840
we then go into the analyze query function.

169
00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:28.000
And this is using the router annotator,

170
00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:32.159
the same way that we saw in the slides just in the previous lesson.

171
00:10:32.719 --> 00:10:37.200
So this indicates to the flow that in here there's going to be a fork on the road,

172
00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:41.119
meaning that depending on the output of this function,

173
00:10:41.119 --> 00:10:45.200
the flow will send to either one or another function.

174
00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:48.159
And this analyzing query function will be executed

175
00:10:48.159 --> 00:10:51.359
as soon as the start conversation function is done.

176
00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:56.080
So in here, you can see that the analyze query does one simple thing.

177
00:10:56.080 --> 00:11:03.200
It basically calls gpt-4o-mini to decide whether it should just give a simple answer

178
00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:08.000
or if it will be required to do a deep research into a topic.

179
00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:11.520
And depending on whether it will do research or not,

180
00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:14.559
it will either emit a research event

181
00:11:14.559 --> 00:11:16.960
or it will emit a simple event.

182
00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:21.280
And you can see on our next function, the simple answer,

183
00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:23.840
that if a simple event is emitted,

184
00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:26.640
then there is a listen decorator for that,

185
00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:29.200
and the simple function will be executed.

186
00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:32.559
And this function then just do one single LLM call

187
00:11:32.559 --> 00:11:35.039
and pushes that answer back into the state.

188
00:11:35.599 --> 00:11:38.080
If it emits a research event,

189
00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:40.960
then we have this clarify query function

190
00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:44.960
that will actually ask the user for a clarifying question,

191
00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:50.000
whether he wants to know something more specific about what it's asking about.

192
00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:52.719
So if it does need a clarifying question,

193
00:11:52.719 --> 00:11:55.280
then the user gets the opportunity to add

194
00:11:55.280 --> 00:11:59.280
what is the additional context in there that it wants to research about.

195
00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:01.520
After the research is done,

196
00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:06.479
we can see that the execute search is now listening to the clarifying query.

197
00:12:07.039 --> 00:12:09.200
And this is we're getting towards the end of the flow.

198
00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:11.760
So bear with me for just one extra second here.

199
00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:12.960
But this is getting interesting

200
00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:15.919
because it's in here that we're going to load our crew.

201
00:12:15.919 --> 00:12:18.320
And you can see that we're loading the same crew

202
00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:21.200
that we built on the last notebook,

203
00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:23.679
the parallel deep research crew.

204
00:12:23.679 --> 00:12:26.400
But now the user query is dynamic.

205
00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:28.559
We're loading that from the state

206
00:12:28.559 --> 00:12:33.359
from what the user inputted early on the other functions that we executed.

207
00:12:33.359 --> 00:12:35.359
And then after this query is done,

208
00:12:35.359 --> 00:12:39.520
you can see that the research report is saved into the state as well.

209
00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:42.159
And then we go into our final function,

210
00:12:42.159 --> 00:12:45.359
that is the save report and summarize,

211
00:12:45.359 --> 00:12:47.039
where basically we're going to make sure

212
00:12:47.039 --> 00:12:52.000
that we are saving the report on this research report markdown file.

213
00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:56.479
We're doing one final LLM call to summarize the content of the report.

214
00:12:56.479 --> 00:13:00.880
And then we go into the save and report and summarize function,

215
00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:03.919
where we have another listening decorator

216
00:13:03.919 --> 00:13:06.799
that is basically waiting for the research to be done.

217
00:13:06.799 --> 00:13:11.039
It's going to save the result into this research report markdown file

218
00:13:11.039 --> 00:13:16.719
and do one single LLM call asking for it to summarize the final report

219
00:13:16.719 --> 00:13:20.159
that will be shown into the state as well as a final answer.

220
00:13:20.799 --> 00:13:26.559
And after this, you can see that our final function is the return final answer.

221
00:13:27.119 --> 00:13:30.400
And this function is listening for multiple events.

222
00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:34.559
So it will listen for either the simple answer event

223
00:13:34.559 --> 00:13:37.919
or the save report and summarize event.

224
00:13:37.919 --> 00:13:40.960
So going back all the way to the beginning of our flow,

225
00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:47.039
if the initial LLM call deemed that it was not required to do a deep research,

226
00:13:47.039 --> 00:13:49.679
this will automatically kick off this function.

227
00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:53.599
If it deemed that it was necessary to do a deep research,

228
00:13:53.599 --> 00:13:55.520
that would go through all the different steps

229
00:13:55.520 --> 00:13:57.440
that we went over up to this point

230
00:13:57.440 --> 00:14:00.320
and eventually get into this final answer function.

231
00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:05.280
And this final answer function basically print out what is the final answer,

232
00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:08.640
whether it's a simple or it's the result of our summary,

233
00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:10.400
and then the entire flow is done.

234
00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:15.039
So you can see that this is just 140 lines of code.

235
00:14:15.039 --> 00:14:19.840
We're just using decorators to control the entire order of the execution.

